WEBVTT

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.180
I have to give a presentation tomorrow

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.120
and I am SO nervous

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:08.130
Yes, I can tell

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.210
especially when you stress the word “SO.”

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.290
What do you mean

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.500
I put emphasis on the word so?

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:20.200 
How does it tell you how I feel?

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.200
When you put stress on a word

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.040
it means the word is important

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.140
You just said I am SO nervous. 

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.260
That shows how nervous you are. 

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.230
I can also put stress on different words

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.090
in that sentence, right?

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:41.050
Yes, you could say “I am so nervous.”

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:43.200 
And “I am so NERVOUS.” 

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.240
Each represents a different meaning

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.200
That’s very interesting

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.050 
And how about “intonation”?

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.110 
It sounds better when you have 

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.110
the correct intonation, right?

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:00.200
Right. Usually a yes/no question

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:03.000
will have a rising intonation

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.120 
“Do you have a new car?” 

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.280
or “Is this his pen?”

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.070
But we usually have a falling intonation 

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:12.190
in a statement sentence

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.020 
such as “Nice to meet you.” 

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.280
We also use falling intonation

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:21.050
in command sentences like, “Give me the key.” 

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:25.220
and in WH-questions like, “What is your name?” 

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.290
So when the intonation changes

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.200 
the sentence has a different meaning

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:32.280
For example

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.220
a statement like, “You have a car.” 

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:39.130
A falling intonation at the end of the sentence 

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.250
means it’s a positive statement. 

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:46.160
You can also have a rising intonation at the end

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:48.040
“You have a car?”

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:51.260 
That might mean you don’t believe the person 

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:54.190
you’re talking to actually has a car

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:58.150
So the intonation can express doubt

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.110
In a presentation, pronunciation is also 

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:05.250
as important as stress and intonation, right? 

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.220
Right. You have to remember that a presentation

00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:12.020
is not just reading your notes

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:15.230
The audience has to feel you’re “talking” to them

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.280
The stress and intonation will help them understand 

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.160
what you mean and what you want them to pay attention to

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.000
And how about rhythm? 

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:27.230
Is that the tempo? 

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.220
Because I am not singing

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.200
Hahaha. Rhythm is not only for music

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:40.000
You can speak fast or slow, soft or loud

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.290
Your voice can be high-pitched or low-pitched

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:47.260 
Also, you need to pause from time to time

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.050
Together, these things make up a rhythm

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:56.080 
If you don’t pay attention and speak in a monotone voice

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.000 
your audience will fall asleep

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:02.170
Wow, that’s so important to know

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:08.110 
I guess I’d better practice my rhythm,stress and intonation.

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:11.050 
Yes, you should! 

